The payload is synthetic, generated by the script below: mostly ARM64
nops, with a branch instruction every 16th and 8th word and a little
incompressible noise, so that both the ARM64 and the x86 filter have
something to convert and PPMd still compresses it down to a few
hundred bytes.

    python3 gen.py 13066 ppmd-arm64.bin

    import struct, sys, zlib
    n = int(sys.argv[1])
    state = 0x2545f491
    out = bytearray()
    while len(out) < n:
        i = len(out) // 4
        pc = len(out)
        if i % 16 == 0:                                          # arm64 bl
            w = 0x94000000 | (((0x40000 - pc) >> 2) & 0x03ffffff)
        elif i % 16 == 8:                                        # x86-ish call
            w = 0x000000e8 | ((pc << 8) & 0xffffff00)
        elif i % 64 == 20:                                       # a little noise
            state = (state * 1103515245 + 12345) & 0xffffffff
            w = state
        else:
            w = 0xd503201f                                       # nop
        out += struct.pack('<I', w)
    data = bytes(out[:n])
    open(sys.argv[2], 'wb').write(data)
    print(len(data), '0x%08x' % (zlib.crc32(data) & 0xffffffff))

rm -f libarchive/test/test_read_format_7zip_ppmd_arm64.7z
7z a -m0=ARM64 -m1=PPMd -mhc=off -mtm=off libarchive/test/test_read_format_7zip_ppmd_arm64.7z ppmd-arm64.bin -y

-mtm=off keeps the result reproducible; the test does not check mtime.

And then uuencoding the resulting archive.

begin 644 test_read_format_7zip_ppmd_arm64.7z
